1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,392
If you run a small clinic or salon,
no-shows cost you real money,

2
00:00:04,442 --> 00:00:05,730
so you take deposits.

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:08,469
Giving the front desk
to an AI is tempting,

4
00:00:08,519 --> 00:00:13,439
but an agent that can charge or refund
on its own is one bad message away from

5
00:00:13,489 --> 00:00:14,650
refunding everyone.

6
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:19,989
Ask First Pay lets the agent do
the routine work with PayPal,

7
00:00:20,039 --> 00:00:24,650
and leaves every decision about
moving money with the owner.

8
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:26,858
A customer books.

9
00:00:26,908 --> 00:00:32,059
The agent creates a PayPal
order with intent AUTHORIZE.

10
00:00:32,109 --> 00:00:38,301
The customer approves it, PayPal's
signed webhook tells the agent,

11
00:00:38,351 --> 00:00:40,949
and the agent authorizes it.

12
00:00:40,999 --> 00:00:47,475
Ten dollars are held, not charged,
and the agent did that on its own,

13
00:00:47,525 --> 00:00:49,650
because no money moved.

14
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:52,542
The customer doesn't show up.

15
00:00:52,592 --> 00:00:56,731
The agent can't capture the
deposit; it can only ask.

16
00:00:56,781 --> 00:01:02,421
The owner's phone shows exactly what
was asked, from the signed mandate,

17
00:01:02,471 --> 00:01:08,506
not from the model's words: capture,
this authorization, at most ten dollars,

18
00:01:08,556 --> 00:01:10,166
single use, expiring.

19
00:01:10,216 --> 00:01:12,537
One tap signs it on the phone.

20
00:01:12,587 --> 00:01:16,094
Only now does the capture
reach PayPal, once,

21
00:01:16,144 --> 00:01:19,650
with the mandate's id
as the idempotency key.

22
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:26,929
What if the agent, or anyone,
tries to use that approval again?

23
00:01:26,979 --> 00:01:30,321
The gate rejects it as a replay,

24
00:01:30,371 --> 00:01:36,894
and the attempt is written to
the ledger like everything else.

25
00:01:36,944 --> 00:01:39,650
One approval, one capture.

26
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,121
And if a customer tries to
talk the agent into a refund?

27
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:46,194
It has no tool that can do it.

28
00:01:46,244 --> 00:01:49,650
Nothing reaches the owner,
nothing reaches PayPal.

29
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:52,596
Another customer cancels.

30
00:01:52,646 --> 00:01:59,541
Because the deposit was only held, the
right move is a void, not a refund.

31
00:01:59,591 --> 00:02:05,641
Releasing a hold is still a
decision, so it also needs the owner,

32
00:02:05,691 --> 00:02:08,362
with a symbolic one-cent cap.

33
00:02:08,412 --> 00:02:14,650
Approved: the authorization is
voided and nothing was ever charged.

34
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:22,562
Every attempt, accepted or rejected,
is in a hash-chained ledger.

35
00:02:22,612 --> 00:02:27,288
Change any row and the chain check breaks.

36
00:02:27,338 --> 00:02:29,650
Anyone can verify it.

37
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:31,729
Ask First Pay.

38
00:02:31,779 --> 00:02:39,650
The agent asks, the owner decides, and
PayPal moves the money exactly once.
